course: dictionary
lesson: 2208
title: Countless
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Countless means so many that they cannot be counted. It can also mean very, very many. When an author writes «there are countless stars», they are not giving a number. They are saying there are far too many to count.
explain: Read the clues in the sentence to check. «In 1610 Galileo turned a small telescope on the Milky Way and found it was made of countless faint stars.» The telescope showed a huge crowd of faint stars, too many to count one by one, so countless fits.
explain: In maths, be careful. «Countless» is not a real number, so you cannot use it in a sum. It is a way of saying a number is huge. A good test is to ask: could I count these, even if it took ages? If the answer is no, countless is the right word.
fact: Countless means so many that they cannot be counted.
fact: «Countless times» means very many times, such as «I have told you countless times».
fact: «Chalk cliffs are made from the shells of countless tiny sea creatures.» The shells were so many that nobody could count them.
fact: Countless is an exaggeration in everyday talk, so it is not an exact number.
fact: Countless is a word for a huge number, so the writer is not giving an exact amount.
exercise: «In 1610 Galileo found the Milky Way was made of countless faint stars.» What does countless mean here? | A small number that is easy to count | *So many they cannot be counted | Made of ice | Sharp and pointy
exercise: «Chalk cliffs are made from the shells of countless tiny sea creatures.» What kind of number does countless show? | *A very, very large number | A number you can count on your fingers | Exactly ten | A number less than zero
exercise: Why is it careful to say «countless stars» in maths? | *Because countless is an exaggeration, not an exact number | Because stars cannot be seen | Because countless means exactly one hundred | Because maths only uses words
test: «Saturn's rings are made of countless chunks of water ice.» What does countless suggest about the chunks? | There are exactly three | They are all the size of a house | There is only one big chunk | *There are so many they cannot be counted
test: Which word is closest in meaning to countless? | *Many | Single | Rare | Few
exam: A child says, «I've told you countless times to tidy your room.» What does countless mean in this sentence? | Exactly one time | Never at all | *Very many times | Only a few times
